  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1019 PM CDT FRI JUL 31 2026\n\nThe leading edge of the trough and cold front will begin to work\ninto the region by tomorrow afternoon, with multiple rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms expected tomorrow. The first round will\nbe sometime during the morning, as a leading shortwave pushes\neast. The main thunderstorm activity will be in the late\nafternoon and evening hours, when the cold front proper begins to\npush into the state. PWAT values will remain very high tomorrow,\nand many of these storms will be prolific rainfall producers. In\nthe event storms start to train, or storm motions slow down,\nlocalized flash flooding can be expected given the high rainfall\nrates. Thankfully, these areas should be limited in scope, and\nwidespread flooding and flash flooding is not anticipated. The\nSPC has also maintained the Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for\ntomorrow, with damaging winds remaining the main threat with any\nstrong to severe thunderstorm activity.\n\nAs the trough and low pressure continue to drop towards the\nsoutheast, it will start to become disconnected from the main\ntrough axis to the north. This now cutoff low will linger across\nthe Ohio Valley into the middle of next week, keeping elevated\nrain chances in the forecast through Wednesday. This will also\naid in keeping afternoon high temperatures suppressed, with highs\nexpected to stay in the upper-80s and low-90s. Unfortunately,\nthis closed low will become absorbed back into the upper-level\nflow by Thursday, as a ridge builds off to the west. This should\nallow for afternoon highs to climb back into the low to mid-90s\nonce more headed into next weekend.\n\n/44/",
